<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.4" snapToGrid="true">
  <code><![CDATA[; Implementation of equations (1)-(9), with a seeded random force per person.
; Coordinates: metres. Velocity: m/s. Acceleration: m/s^2.
; Interface variables: population, dt, desired-speed, noise-strength, noise-seed.

breed [pedestrians pedestrian]

globals [
  tau anticipation-time potential-strength sigma
  wall-strength wall-range view-half-angle rear-weight
  epsilon arrival-radius wall-segments elapsed-seconds arrived-count
]

pedestrians-own [
  goal-x goal-y                 ; destination x_i^0
  ex ey                         ; desired unit direction e_i
  wx wy                         ; preferred velocity w_i (kept between steps)
  vx vy                         ; actual, speed-limited velocity v_i
  ax ay                         ; acceleration dw_i/dt
  noise-unit-x noise-unit-y     ; random unit direction, sampled once at setup
  next-wx next-wy next-vx next-vy next-x next-y
  reached-goal?
]

to setup
  clear-all
  resize-world 0 30 0 12
  set-topology false false

  ; Example settings for quantities left unspecified in the report.
  set tau 0.5
  set anticipation-time 0.5     ; T, independent of numerical step dt
  set potential-strength 2.1   ; V^0, units m^2/s^2
  set sigma 0.3                ; interaction range, metres
  set view-half-angle 100      ; phi; NetLogo trigonometry uses degrees
  set rear-weight 0.5          ; c
  set epsilon 0.000001         ; length softening at singular geometries
  set arrival-radius 0.3       ; metres

  ; Equation (4): values explicitly given in your report.
  set wall-strength 50
  set wall-range 0.2
  ; Every wall is [x1 y1 x2 y2]. Corridor is open at both ends.
  ; Grey patches end at y=1.5 and begin at y=10.5.
  set wall-segments [[0 1.5 30 1.5] [0 10.5 30 10.5]]
  ask patches [
    set pcolor white
    if pycor <= 1 or pycor >= 11 [set pcolor gray - 2]
    if (pxcor = 1 or pxcor = 29) and pycor > 1 and pycor < 11
      [set pcolor green + 3]
  ]

  create-pedestrians population [
    set shape "circle"
    set size 0.4               ; drawing only; no radius in the equations
    let group-index floor (who / 2)
    let lane-count min (list 4 (population / 2))
    let row-index group-index mod lane-count
    let column-index floor (group-index / lane-count)
    let lane-y 6 + 2 * (row-index - (lane-count - 1) / 2)
    ; Opposing pairs have exactly the same y coordinate and goal-y.
    ; There is no starting offset; the random force can break this symmetry.
    ifelse who mod 2 = 0 [
      setxy (10 - 1.5 * column-index) lane-y
      set goal-x 29
      set color orange + 1
      set heading 90
    ] [
      setxy (20 + 1.5 * column-index) lane-y
      set goal-x 1
      set color blue
      set heading 270
    ]
    set goal-y ycor
    set reached-goal? false
    set pen-size 1
    pen-down                   ; leave trails to show the resulting paths
    ; All velocity components initially equal zero.
  ]
  ; The report's f_i^r is assigned once, not resampled every tick.
  ; Seed just this sampling step to make it independent of setup's ask order.
  random-seed noise-seed
  foreach sort pedestrians [p -> ask p [
    let noise-angle random-float 360
    set noise-unit-x cos noise-angle
    set noise-unit-y sin noise-angle
  ]]
  ask pedestrians [update-desired-direction]
  reset-ticks
end

to go
  if not any? pedestrians [stop]
  if dt <= 0 or tau <= 0 or sigma <= 0 or wall-range <= 0
    [error "dt, tau, sigma and wall-range must be positive."]

  ; Four separate passes ensure everyone uses the same time-t state.
  ; Ordered passes also avoid introducing random ask order.
  foreach sort pedestrians [p -> ask p [update-desired-direction]]
  foreach sort pedestrians [p -> ask p [calculate-acceleration]]
  foreach sort pedestrians [p -> ask p [calculate-next-state]]
  foreach sort pedestrians [p -> ask p [commit-next-state]]
  set elapsed-seconds elapsed-seconds + dt
  tick                        ; one tick = one integration step
end

; Equation (1).
to update-desired-direction
  let delta-x goal-x - xcor
  let delta-y goal-y - ycor
  let distance-to-goal vector-length delta-x delta-y
  ifelse distance-to-goal > epsilon [
    set ex delta-x / distance-to-goal
    set ey delta-y / distance-to-goal
  ] [
    set ex 0
    set ey 0
  ]
end

; Equations (2), (4), (5), (6).
to calculate-acceleration
  set ax (desired-speed * ex - vx) / tau
  set ay (desired-speed * ey - vy) / tau

  foreach sort other pedestrians [other-person ->
    let force pair-force other-person
    let fx item 0 force
    let fy item 1 force
    let magnitude vector-length fx fy
    let weight rear-weight
    ; Equation (5) uses MINUS the force in its dot product.
    if ex * (- fx) + ey * (- fy) >= magnitude * cos view-half-angle
      [set weight 1]
    set ax ax + weight * fx
    set ay ay + weight * fy
  ]

  foreach wall-segments [wall ->
    let force wall-force wall
    set ax ax + item 0 force
    set ay ay + item 1 force
  ]
  ; Equation (6): constant random force while noise-strength is held fixed.
  ; Strength is acceleration (m/s^2), not a displacement applied each tick.
  set ax ax + noise-strength * noise-unit-x
  set ay ay + noise-strength * noise-unit-y
end

; Equation (3), using its analytic negative gradient.
to-report pair-force [other-person]
  let qx xcor - [xcor] of other-person
  let qy ycor - [ycor] of other-person
  let other-speed vector-length ([vx] of other-person) ([vy] of other-person)
  ; s_j uses ACTUAL speed and DESIRED direction, exactly as in the PDF.
  let sx other-speed * anticipation-time * [ex] of other-person
  let sy other-speed * anticipation-time * [ey] of other-person
  report elliptical-force qx qy sx sy
end

to-report elliptical-force [qx qy sx sy]
  let rx qx - sx
  let ry qy - sy
  ; Softened lengths prevent division by zero at the ellipse's foci.
  ; Away from singularities the change is negligible (epsilon = 1e-6 m).
  let r1 sqrt (qx * qx + qy * qy + epsilon * epsilon)
  let r2 sqrt (rx * rx + ry * ry + epsilon * epsilon)
  let total-distance r1 + r2
  let b 0.5 * sqrt (max (list (4 * epsilon * epsilon)
    (total-distance * total-distance - sx * sx - sy * sy)))
  let scale (potential-strength / sigma) * exp (- b / sigma)
    * total-distance / (4 * b)
  report (list (scale * (qx / r1 + rx / r2))
               (scale * (qy / r1 + ry / r2)))
end

; Equation (4), for the nearest point on a finite wall segment.
to-report wall-force [wall]
  let nearest nearest-point-on-segment xcor ycor wall
  let delta-x xcor - item 0 nearest
  let delta-y ycor - item 1 nearest
  let d vector-length delta-x delta-y
  ; The outward direction is undefined exactly on a zero-thickness wall.
  if d < epsilon [error "Pedestrian is exactly on a wall. Check its position or reduce dt."]
  let strength wall-strength * exp (- d / wall-range)
  report (list (strength * delta-x / d) (strength * delta-y / d))
end

; Equations (7), (8), (9). Write buffers, not the current state.
to calculate-next-state
  set next-wx wx + dt * ax
  set next-wy wy + dt * ay
  let preferred-speed vector-length next-wx next-wy
  let speed-factor 1
  if preferred-speed > 0 [
    set speed-factor min (list 1 (1.3 * desired-speed / preferred-speed))
  ]
  set next-vx next-wx * speed-factor
  set next-vy next-wy * speed-factor
  set next-x xcor + dt * next-vx
  set next-y ycor + dt * next-vy

  ; Arrive if this step's path enters the goal disk, even if it overshoots.
  let path (list xcor ycor next-x next-y)
  let closest nearest-point-on-segment goal-x goal-y path
  set reached-goal? (vector-length (goal-x - item 0 closest)
                                    (goal-y - item 1 closest)) <= arrival-radius
  if not reached-goal? [
    if next-x <= min-pxcor - 0.5 or next-x >= max-pxcor + 0.5 or
       next-y <= min-pycor - 0.5 or next-y >= max-pycor + 0.5 [
      error "A step would leave the world. Reduce dt or review forces and destinations."
    ]
  ]
end

to commit-next-state
  if reached-goal? [
    set arrived-count arrived-count + 1
    die
  ]
  set wx next-wx
  set wy next-wy
  set vx next-vx
  set vy next-vy
  setxy next-x next-y
  if vector-length vx vy > epsilon [set heading atan vx vy]
end

to-report vector-length [delta-x delta-y]
  report sqrt (delta-x * delta-x + delta-y * delta-y)
end

to-report nearest-point-on-segment [px py segment]
  let x1 item 0 segment
  let y1 item 1 segment
  let delta-x (item 2 segment) - x1
  let delta-y (item 3 segment) - y1
  let length-squared delta-x * delta-x + delta-y * delta-y
  if length-squared = 0 [report (list x1 y1)]
  let fraction ((px - x1) * delta-x + (py - y1) * delta-y) / length-squared
  set fraction max (list 0 (min (list 1 fraction)))
  report (list (x1 + fraction * delta-x) (y1 + fraction * delta-y))
end

to-report average-speed
  if not any? pedestrians [report 0]
  report mean [vector-length vx vy] of pedestrians
end
]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="265" y="10" width="840" height="354" patchSize="27" minPxcor="0" maxPxcor="30" minPycor="0" maxPycor="12" wrappingAllowedX="false" wrappingAllowedY="false" frameRate="30" fontSize="12" showTickCounter="true" tickCounterLabel="steps" updateMode="1" />
    <button x="10" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="setup" kind="Observer" forever="false" disableUntilTicks="false">setup</button>
    <button x="90" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="go" kind="Observer" forever="true" disableUntilTicks="true">go</button>
    <button x="170" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="step" kind="Observer" forever="false" disableUntilTicks="true">go</button>
    <slider x="10" y="65" width="235" height="50" display="population" variable="population" min="2" max="48" step="2" default="2" direction="Horizontal" units="people" />
    <slider x="10" y="125" width="235" height="50" display="dt" variable="dt" min="0.005" max="0.1" step="0.005" default="0.02" direction="Horizontal" units="s" />
    <slider x="10" y="185" width="235" height="50" display="desired-speed" variable="desired-speed" min="0.2" max="2.0" step="0.02" default="1.34" direction="Horizontal" units="m/s" />
    <monitor x="10" y="380" width="115" height="55" display="simulated seconds" precision="2" fontSize="11">elapsed-seconds</monitor>
    <monitor x="130" y="380" width="115" height="55" display="mean speed (m/s)" precision="3" fontSize="11">average-speed</monitor>
    <monitor x="10" y="445" width="115" height="55" display="walking" precision="0" fontSize="11">count pedestrians</monitor>
    <monitor x="130" y="445" width="115" height="55" display="arrived" precision="0" fontSize="11">arrived-count</monitor>
    <note x="275" y="380" width="740" height="110" fontSize="13" markdown="false" backgroundLight="0" backgroundDark="0" textColorLight="-16777216" textColorDark="-1">HEAD-ON TEST WITH RANDOM FORCE
Both pedestrians start at exactly y = 6.
noise-strength = 0 turns the random force off.
Change noise-seed, then setup, for another sample.
Trails show whether and how they pass each other.</note>
    <slider x="10" y="250" width="235" height="50" display="noise-strength" variable="noise-strength" min="0" max="0.2" step="0.005" default="0.05" direction="Horizontal" units="m/s^2" />
    <slider x="10" y="310" width="235" height="50" display="noise-seed" variable="noise-seed" min="0" max="100" step="1" default="42" direction="Horizontal" units="setup" />
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## START HERE

Click setup, then go. Click go again to pause. Use step to advance once.
Orange pedestrians walk right; blue pedestrians walk left. They disappear
when their path reaches a disk of radius 0.3 m around their destination.
Starting positions, destinations and desired speed stay fixed. Each pedestrian
gets a random force direction at setup; the direction is then kept constant.
Use setup after changing population or noise-seed. dt, desired-speed and
noise-strength take effect immediately.
Start with population = 2, dt = 0.02 s and desired-speed = 1.34 m/s.
The pair starts at (10, 6) and (20, 6), walking directly towards each other.
Their destinations are (29, 6) and (1, 6). There is no lateral offset.
The small random force breaks the head-on symmetry; trails show the detour.
Start with noise-strength = 0.05 m/s^2 and noise-seed = 42. This seed repeats
the same force directions on every setup. Change noise-seed and click setup
for another sample. Set noise-strength = 0 to recover the symmetric stall.
This force is a persistent individual bias, not fresh jitter every tick.
Increase population for multiple opposing pairs in up to four shared lanes.
For a comparison without pedestrian repulsion, run setup and enter
"set potential-strength 0" in the Command Center before clicking go.
Click setup to restore the original strength for the next comparison.

## UNITS AND TIME

One coordinate unit (one patch width) is one metre. Pedestrians use continuous
xcor/ycor positions, not patch-to-patch jumps. All forces act as accelerations.
One tick is one numerical step; elapsed-seconds accumulates dt. Neither depends
on the computer's wall clock. T = anticipation-time is separate from dt.

## EQUATION TO PROCEDURE MAP

1: update-desired-direction computes [ex ey].
2: calculate-acceleration starts with (desired-speed * e - v) / tau.
3: pair-force constructs q_ij and s_j; elliptical-force evaluates -grad V.
4: wall-force finds the nearest point on each finite wall segment.
5: calculate-acceleration weights each pedestrian force using e dot (-f).
6: calculate-acceleration sums the driving, pedestrian, wall and random-force terms.
7: calculate-next-state integrates preferred velocity [wx wy].
8: calculate-next-state limits actual velocity [vx vy] to 1.3 * desired-speed.
9: calculate-next-state advances position with the NEW actual velocity.

For Eq. 3, let r1 = ||q||, r2 = ||q-s|| and R = r1+r2. Differentiating
with s held fixed gives:

    grad b = R / (4*b) * (q/r1 + (q-s)/r2)
    f = (V0/sigma) * exp(-b/sigma) * grad b

The code uses sqrt(length-squared + epsilon-squared) for r1 and r2 and
a roundoff floor for b. This smooths the undefined foci with epsilon = 1e-6 m.
At exact coincident positions a smoothed force can be zero: this is a point-agent
model, not a hard-body collision solver. Initial positions are separated.

## UPDATE ORDER

go has four passes: update all desired directions, calculate all accelerations,
calculate all next states, then commit all next states. No pedestrian sees a
neighbour's updated position or velocity during force calculation. wx/wy are
stored separately from vx/vy and are NOT reset to the capped velocity.
The loops use sorted agent lists to keep the calculation order deterministic.
All random directions are sampled at setup using noise-seed. No random
numbers are sampled while go runs. With fixed controls, a repeated setup
reproduces the same trajectory.

## PARAMETERS

The interface exposes population, dt, desired-speed, noise-strength and
noise-seed. noise-strength is the magnitude of f_i^r in m/s^2. A uniform
angle from 0 to 360 degrees gives its direction; this choice is not calibrated
to data. It remains constant per person while the strength slider is fixed.
Other parameters are set near the start of setup, in the Code tab:

    tau = 0.5 s
    anticipation-time (T) = 0.5 s
    potential-strength (V0) = 2.1 m^2/s^2
    sigma = 0.3 m
    view-half-angle (phi) = 100 degrees
    rear-weight (c) = 0.5
    wall-strength (AW) = 50 m/s^2
    wall-range (BW) = 0.2 m

AW and BW are stated in your report. Values for the other interaction
parameters are example settings; they are not fitted to data here.
NetLogo's sin/cos/atan use its degree and heading conventions:
https://docs.netlogo.org/dict/atan

## GEOMETRY AND LIMITATIONS

The example is a 30 m corridor with walls at y=1.5 and y=10.5, open at both
ends. wall-segments stores each wall as [x1 y1 x2 y2]; change these coordinates
to change the force geometry. Patch colours only illustrate this corridor;
changing colours alone does not change the walls. Wrapping is disabled.
Destinations are points at x=1 or x=29, initially at each pedestrian's own y.

Repulsion is soft. There is no extra collision force, radius offset, reflection,
route planner or attraction term in the report's equations, so none is added.
The circle size is purely visual. Very large timesteps can cross a wall or
destabilize the calculation. Compare dt = 0.02 and 0.01 at equal simulated
time before trusting a quantitative result. Leaving the NetLogo world raises
an explanatory error rather than silently wrapping or clamping a position.
All pedestrians currently share desired-speed; individual speeds can be added later.

## SOURCE

Implements equations (1)-(9) from the supplied IKT467 pedestrian report,
including the report's once-assigned random force in Eq. (6). The fixed
starting geometry, uniform force direction, chosen force magnitude, arrival
rule and numerical regularization are explicit implementation choices.
]]></info>
  <turtleShapes>
    <shape name="default" rotatable="false" editableColorIndex="0">
      <circle x="0" y="0" diameter="300" filled="true" marked="true" color="-1920102913" />
    </shape>
    <shape name="circle" rotatable="false" editableColorIndex="0">
      <circle x="0" y="0" diameter="300" filled="true" marked="true" color="-1920102913" />
    </shape>
  </turtleShapes>
  <linkShapes />
  <previewCommands>setup repeat 250 [go]</previewCommands>
</model>